adjudication.rationale:
In EventManager.ts the refactor changes a null-safe check to:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar")
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `null`, `undefined`, or an empty array, destructuring yields `mainHostDestinationCalendar === undefined`, and accessing `.integration` will throw at runtime. Previously it used optional chaining (`evt.destinationCalendar?.integration`), which could not crash and would correctly trigger the fallback. This is a real null-safety regression introduced by the diff.

Yes. The diff changes the Meet fallback check from optional chaining to destructuring:

```ts
const [mainHostDestinationCalendar] = evt.destinationCalendar ?? [];
if (evt.location === MeetLocationType && mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration !== "google_calendar")
```

If `evt.destinationCalendar` is `undefined`, `null`, or an empty array, `mainHostDestinationCalendar` will be `undefined`, and dereferencing `mainHostDestinationCalendar.integration` will throw a `TypeError`. This can happen for bookings with Google Meet location but no destination calendar, whereas the prior code (`evt.destinationCalendar?.integration`) was safe and would fall back to Cal Video.
